# Security-owned paths.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS NOW RATHER THAN AT CENTRALIZATION
#
# `gate_mode: log-only` makes the gate genuinely non-blocking — including for
# report-integrity failures. The caller workflows are app-team-owned and edited
# through ordinary pull requests, so without this file a one-line change to
# `.github/workflows/security.yml` turns a red required check green, reviewed by
# whoever normally reviews that repo's code. That is an unreviewed path to
# bypassing the repo's own security gate.
#
# These paths therefore require review from the security owner:
#   - the callers, where `gate_mode` and the reusable-workflow references live
#   - the reusable workflows themselves
#   - the policy, the gate scripts, and the committed Semgrep baseline
#
# THIS FILE ALONE ENFORCES NOTHING. CODEOWNERS is advisory until branch
# protection on `main` enables "Require review from Code Owners" (and a review
# count of at least 1). See docs/onboarding.md § "Who can turn the gate off".

# Everything CI executes.
/.github/workflows/   @IamRitz
/.github/CODEOWNERS   @IamRitz

# The policy the gate enforces, the code that enforces it, and the accepted-
# findings baseline it measures against.
/security/policy.yaml        @IamRitz
/security/scripts/           @IamRitz
/security/baseline/          @IamRitz
/security/semgrep-rules.yml  @IamRitz

# In a real org these would be a team (e.g. @your-org/security-engineering)
# rather than an individual, so review does not depend on one person's
# availability.
